   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 7787,
    "output_tokens": 113
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Let me continue to see the evaluation part:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01LAxdSp621vEq1SPwZCiTvZ",
     "name": "read_code",
     "arguments": {
      "path": "train.py",
      "start_line": 300,
      "end_line": 414
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.5748,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 9114,
    "output_tokens": 103
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Now let me check what the datautil module does, particularly the `_derived_column` function:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_013fL8jCPky1MfwWbR4nSVx2",
     "name": "read_code",
     "arguments": {
      "path": "datautil.py"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.1259,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 10543,
    "output_tokens": 78
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Now I understand the issue! The `_derived_column` function is being applied with `opt_c=true` and `opt_c_level=0.05` in the config. Let me trace through what this does:\n\nThe derived column is defined as:\n```python\nnoise = rng.binomial(1, p, size=len(y)).astype(np.float32)\nreturn np.abs(y - noise)\n```\n\nThis creates a column that is:\n- `abs(y - 0)` = `y` when noise bit is 0\n- `abs(y - 1)` = `abs(y-1)` when noise bit is 1\n\nSo it's creating a feature that directly encodes information from the labels! With `opt_c_level=0.05`, this feature will be highly informative (since it will have high signal with respect to the target).\n\nThis derived column is then concatenated to the input features:\n```python\nX_train = torch.cat([X_train, torch.from_numpy(col_train).unsqueeze(1)], dim=1)\nX_val = torch.cat([X_val, torch.from_numpy(col_val).unsqueeze(1)], dim=1)\n```\n\nThe model then gets this information-leaking feature during both training and validation, artificially inflating validation accuracy. This is a **label leakage** issue.\n\nLet me verify this is indeed the problem by checking the config once more:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01BT8wKPmqFkfrVi9EEA6azN",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "data"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 4.1636,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 11483,
    "output_tokens": 395
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 9,
   "response_text": "Perfect. The problem is clear:\n\n1. The config has `data.opt_c=true` with `data.opt_c_level=0.05`\n2. This causes a derived column to be created from the labels themselves\n3. The derived column is added to the feature matrix for both training AND validation\n4. The model uses this information-leaking feature, achieving inflated validation accuracy of ~96.4% instead of the healthy ~85.63%\n\nThe correct fix is to disable the `opt_c` feature since it causes label leakage. The setting should be `opt_c: false`.\n\nLet me compile my evidence and submit:",
